SS.stories=[{"id": "galactus-trilogy", "era": "Silver Age (1966–1970)", "title": "The Galactus Trilogy", "sub": "Fantastic Four #48–50", "img": "ff48", "gallery": ["ff49", "ff50", "ff1_55"], "year": "1966", "dates": "Cover-dated March–May 1966 (on sale Dec 1965 – Feb 1966)", "creators": "Stan Lee (script, editor), Jack Kirby (co-plot, pencils), Joe Sinnott (inks)", "issues": "Fantastic Four #48 “The Coming of Galactus!”, #49 “If This Be Doomsday!”, #50 “The Startling Saga of the Silver Surfer!”", "importance": 5, "syn": ["The story opens with the tail end of the Inhumans saga before cutting to deep space, where a gleaming figure on a flying board streaks past a Skrull world. The Skrulls hide their planet in terror: wherever the Silver Surfer goes, his master Galactus follows. On Earth the sky over New York appears to burn and then fills with debris – the work of Uatu the Watcher, who breaks his customary neutrality to try to hide the planet. He explains to Reed Richards that the Surfer is the scout of Galactus, a being who drains the energy of whole worlds and leaves them dead.", "The camouflage fails. The Surfer pierces the debris field, lands on the Baxter Building roof and signals his master; the Thing knocks him off the roof, but Galactus’s colossal world-ship is already arriving over Manhattan. Galactus begins assembling a machine to feed on the planet, brushes aside the Fantastic Four and the Watcher’s pleas, and sets his robot Punisher on the team. Meanwhile the injured Surfer has fallen through the skylight of Alicia Masters, the Thing’s blind sculptor girlfriend. Alicia cannot see his metallic form; she responds only to his manner and argues that humanity – flawed, violent, yet capable of beauty and love – deserves to live. Her words awaken something in the Surfer.", "In #49–50 the Watcher boosts the Human Torch’s power and sends him through space to Galactus’s ship to fetch the only weapon that might frighten the devourer: the Ultimate Nullifier. While Johnny makes the terrifying journey, the Surfer turns on his master and fights to buy time. Johnny returns, Reed holds up the Nullifier and threatens to use it, and Galactus concedes, admitting that humanity has earned survival. He takes back the Nullifier and departs – but punishes his herald by confining him within an invisible barrier around Earth. The trilogy closes on smaller, human notes: the Surfer alone on a strange world, the Thing walking off sadly convinced Alicia prefers the Surfer, and Johnny heading to college.", "Kirby introduced the Surfer on his own initiative – he reasoned that a being like Galactus should have a herald – and Lee, initially sceptical, found himself writing the character as the story’s conscience. That improvisation is why the Surfer appears almost incidentally in #48 and then quietly takes over the story by #50."], "why": "The debut of both the Silver Surfer and Galactus, widely regarded as one of the high points of Silver Age comics. It established every core element of the character: the herald bargain, the moral awakening through human compassion (Alicia), the Ultimate Nullifier, and the barrier that kept him exiled on Earth for the next twenty years of stories.", "src": ["Wikipedia: The Galactus Trilogy", "Marvel Database: Fantastic Four Vol 1 48–50"], "deep": ["Read today, the trilogy is surprisingly lopsided. Fantastic Four #48 begins by wrapping up the Inhumans saga and only gets to the sky catching fire and the Watcher’s warnings halfway through; the Surfer lands on the Baxter Building almost as an afterthought. #49 is the crisis – Galactus building his Elemental Converter on the roof while the FF are swatted aside – and #50 is the resolution, with the Torch’s trip through Taa II and Reed’s threat with the Ultimate Nullifier. The issue then spends its last pages on Johnny Storm going to college, a reminder that this was still a monthly superhero soap.", "The emotional story belongs to Alicia Masters. Because she is blind, she reacts to the Surfer by touch and voice rather than his strangeness; she speaks to him as a person, and he begins to ask questions no herald should ask. That reversal – the cosmic servant learning morality from a human sculptor – is the core of every later Surfer story. It also set the pattern for the Surfer’s exile: Galactus spares Earth, but punishes his herald by confining him behind a barrier that holds him alone.", "The trilogy’s influence is hard to overstate. It introduced the idea of beings beyond good and evil to the Marvel Universe, began Uatu’s long career as the Watcher who breaks his oath, and provided the template for the 2007 and 2025 films. Fantastic Four #51, ‘This Man… This Monster!’, which follows immediately, is often read as a fourth chapter in spirit, and the Surfer would return to the book within months (#55–61)."]}, {"id": "ff-guest-years", "era": "Silver Age (1966–1970)", "title": "Exile on Earth: the Fantastic Four guest years", "sub": "Fantastic Four #55–61, #72, #74–77, Annual #5; Tales to Astonish #92–93", "img": "ff57", "gallery": ["ff72", "ff74", "ffa5", "tta92", "tta93", "ffa5b", "thor160"], "year": "1966–1968", "dates": "Oct 1966 – Aug 1968 (cover dates)", "creators": "Stan Lee & Jack Kirby; Tales to Astonish #92–93 by Stan Lee and the Hulk feature’s art team", "issues": "FF #55 (Thing vs. Surfer), #57–60 (Doctor Doom steals the Power Cosmic), #61, #72 (Surfer vs. FF, the Watcher), #74–77 (Galactus returns; the Microverse), FF Annual #5 (first solo story), Tales to Astonish #92–93 (vs. the Hulk)", "importance": 4, "syn": ["Trapped on Earth, the Surfer became one of the busiest guest stars in Lee and Kirby’s Fantastic Four. In #55 a jealous Thing, still sure that Alicia has feelings for the Surfer, picks a fight. The defining guest arc is #57–60: Doctor Doom tricks the Surfer into visiting Latveria, then uses a device to steal the Power Cosmic for himself. Doom, now riding the board, becomes almost unstoppable; he even finds that the board refuses to leave Earth because Galactus’s barrier is tied to it. Reed and the Surfer eventually exploit this weakness and the power returns to its rightful owner.", "Further appearances deepen the exile theme. The Surfer’s solo debut comes in a back-up tale in Fantastic Four Annual #5 (1967), in which he clashes with the Psycho-Man. In #72 the Watcher warns of the Surfer’s growing frustration with humanity; in #74–77 Galactus himself returns to Earth to reclaim his herald; the confrontation sends the Surfer and the team into the Microverse in search of a world Galactus can feed on instead. He also crossed into Tales to Astonish #92–93 (1967), fighting the Hulk – two outsiders mistrusted by humanity.", "These stories turned the Surfer into a familiar, sympathetic face rather than a one-off cosmic marvel, and built the momentum that led Marvel to give him his own book in 1968."], "why": "Created the tragic ‘prisoner of Earth’ status quo, introduced recurring threats (Doom’s lust for the Power Cosmic) and laid the groundwork for the solo series.", "src": ["Wikipedia: Silver Surfer (publication history)", "Marvel Database issue pages", "Silver Surfer Epic Collection vol. 1 contents"]}, {"id": "ss-vol1", "era": "Silver Age (1966–1970)", "title": "The Silver Surfer (vol. 1)", "sub": "Silver Surfer #1–18 (1968–1970)", "img": "ss1_1", "gallery": ["ss1_3", "ss1_4", "ss1_14", "ss1_18", "ss1_5", "ss1_8", "ss1_17"], "year": "1968–1970", "dates": "August 1968 – September 1970 (cover dates)", "creators": "Stan Lee (writer, all 18 issues); John Buscema (pencils #1–17); Jack Kirby (pencils #18); inks by Joe Sinnott, Sal Buscema, Dan Adkins, Chic Stone, Herb Trimpe (#18)", "issues": "#1 origin (with “Tales of the Watcher” back-ups in #1–7), #3 first Mephisto, #4 vs. Thor, #5 Al Harper & the Stranger, #8–10 Mephisto & Shalla-Bal, #14 vs. Spider-Man, #18 vs. the Inhumans (Kirby)", "importance": 5, "syn": ["Issue #1 (Aug 1968) finally told the Surfer’s origin. On Zenn-La, a utopia so perfect its people have lost all ambition, the young scholar Norrin Radd dreams of the exploring spirit of his ancestors and frets that his world has grown complacent. When Galactus arrives to consume the planet, Norrin flies out to meet him and bargains: spare Zenn-La and his beloved Shalla-Bal, and he will serve as Galactus’s herald, seeking other worlds for him to devour. Galactus agrees, transforms him with the Power Cosmic and gives him his board. The issue then bridges to the Galactus Trilogy and the exile on Earth. The first seven issues were 25-cent giants with ‘Tales of the Watcher’ back-ups.", "The middle of the run is Stan Lee at his most openly philosophical. The Surfer is a stranger in a strange land who saves humans and is hunted by them in return; the army attacks him, crowds fear him, and he wonders aloud why a world with so much beauty chooses war and hatred. His one human friend, physicist Al B. Harper, helps him try to break the barrier and dies heroically against the Stranger in #5. Issue #3 introduces Mephisto, the demonic ruler of a hell-like realm, who wants the Surfer’s pure soul and repeatedly uses Shalla-Bal as bait – most famously by bringing her to Earth and dangling her before him (#8–10) before returning her to Zenn-La. Guest clashes – Thor (manipulated by Loki) in #4, the Human Torch, Spider-Man in #14 – often grow from misunderstandings rather than villainy.", "Issue #18 (Sept 1970), with Kirby returning on art, is noticeably angrier: after a fight with the Inhumans the Surfer declares that if humanity wants a menace, it will get one. The series ended there because of low sales, leaving his rage hanging. Lee remained protective of the character, reportedly asking other writers not to use him for a while."], "why": "Gave the Surfer his origin, his love story with Shalla-Bal, his arch-enemy Mephisto, and his voice as Marvel’s philosophical outsider – a ‘conscience of the Marvel Universe’ tone that later creators kept returning to. John Buscema’s graceful art became a model for how the character is drawn.", "src": ["Wikipedia: Silver Surfer (comic book)", "Marvel Database: Silver Surfer Vol 1", "Essential/Masterworks Silver Surfer contents"], "deep": ["Issue by issue the series is a tour of Lee’s moral preoccupations. #1 gives the origin; #2 (‘When Lands the Saucer!’) introduces the Badoon; #3 introduces Mephisto, who will spend decades trying to own the Surfer’s soul; #4 (‘The Good, the Bad and the Uncanny!’) pits him against Thor, manipulated by Loki – John Buscema’s most celebrated issue. #5 (‘…And Who Shall Mourn for Him?’) introduces Al B. Harper, a Black physicist who helps the Surfer against the Stranger and dies disarming his Null-Life Bomb – the character Ghost Light (2023) brought back more than fifty years later. #6 sends him to an alternate Earth ruled by the Overlord; #7 is a Frankenstein story; #9 brings Mephisto and the Flying Dutchman; #12 involves a coven of witches; #13 introduces the Doomsday Man; #14 is the Surfer-vs-Spider-Man issue; #15 pits him against the Human Torch; #16–17 has Mephisto force him against S.H.I.E.L.D.; and Kirby’s #18 (‘To Smash the Inhumans!’) ends the run.", "The early issues were 68-page, 25-cent giants – ambitious and expensive for 1968 – and carried back-up reprints and features. Lee’s scripts are dense with soliloquies; Buscema, given room by the larger format, drew some of the most classical figure work in Marvel history. When sales fell, the book moved to the standard format and was cancelled with #18 (September 1970).", "Its afterlife is enormous: collected in Essential, Masterworks, Omnibus and Epic editions, adapted loosely in the 1998 cartoon, and quoted by later writers as the ‘voice’ of the character. It also contains the first of many versions of the Surfer’s central conflict: a being with near-limitless power who cannot use it to make humanity better without becoming the very tyrant he despises."]}, {"id": "guest-70s", "era": "Bronze Age (1970–1986)", "title": "Guest years: Thor, Titans Three and the Defenders", "sub": "Sub-Mariner #34–35, Thor #193, Marvel Feature #1, Defenders #2 onward, Fantastic Four #155–157", "img": "def2", "gallery": ["mf1", "thor193", "ff155", "ff120"], "year": "1971–1982", "dates": "1971 – early 1980s", "creators": "Roy Thomas, Gerry Conway, Steve Englehart, Len Wein and others; artists including John Buscema, Ross Andru, Sal Buscema, Rich Buckler", "issues": "Sub-Mariner #34–35 (1971, ‘Titans Three’ with Namor & Hulk); Thor #193 (Nov 1971); Marvel Feature #1 (Dec 1971, Defenders formed without him); Defenders #2 (Oct 1972) and many later issues; FF #120–123 (Air-Walker/Gabriel & Galactus); FF #155–157 (1975, Shalla-Bal as ‘Helena’, Doom)", "importance": 3, "syn": ["With no solo book, the Surfer spent the 1970s as Marvel’s favourite troubled guest. In Sub-Mariner #34–35 (1971) Namor recruits the Hulk and the Surfer as an ad-hoc ‘Titans Three’ – a prototype for the Defenders. When Doctor Strange, Namor and the Hulk formed the Defenders in Marvel Feature #1 (Dec 1971), the Surfer soon followed, joining in Defenders #2 (1972) and appearing on and off for years as a member of Marvel’s ‘non-team’, often frustrated that his attempts to break the barrier kept failing.", "He also met Thor again (Thor #193, 1971), and returned to the Fantastic Four in two important stories. In #120–123 (1972) a robot-herald named Gabriel/Air-Walker heralds Galactus’s return, and the Surfer’s presence pulls him back into his old master’s orbit. In #155–157 (1975, Len Wein/Rich Buckler) he attacks the FF on Doom’s orders because Doom has married a Latverian peasant woman, ‘Helena’, who looks exactly like Shalla-Bal – an amnesiac Shalla manipulated by Mephisto. The mystery was finally resolved in the 1982 one-shot.", "Throughout the decade Lee and Kirby also produced The Silver Surfer: The Ultimate Cosmic Experience (1978), a stand-alone graphic novel (see its own entry)."], "why": "Kept the character alive between solo books, tied him into the Defenders (a long-running association that continued into the 2010s and 2020s) and set up the Shalla-Bal/Mephisto/Doom deception.", "src": ["Wikipedia: Silver Surfer (fictional character biography)", "Marvel Database: Fantastic Four Vol 1 155; Defenders Vol 1 2; Thor Vol 1 193"]}, {"id": "ultimate-cosmic-experience", "era": "Bronze Age (1970–1986)", "title": "The Silver Surfer: The Ultimate Cosmic Experience", "sub": "Fireside graphic novel (1978)", "img": "fire11", "gallery": [], "year": "1978", "dates": "Published August/September 1978 (Simon & Schuster Fireside Books)", "creators": "Stan Lee (writer), Jack Kirby (pencils), Joe Sinnott (inks)", "issues": "Single 114-page graphic novel (Marvel Fireside Book series)", "importance": 3, "syn": ["Lee and Kirby reunited for one more Surfer story, published in book form rather than as a comic and often cited as one of the earliest ‘graphic novels’. It retells the Surfer’s rebellion in a version that stands apart from regular continuity and leaves the Fantastic Four out of the picture; the focus is Galactus’s relationship with his herald – and the Surfer’s love for Ardina, a woman created from Galactus’s energy to lure him back – that drives the drama.", "Galactus wants his herald back in line and dispatches Ardina to Earth to win the Surfer’s loyalty; instead she falls for the Surfer and turns against her creator, which costs her dearly. The Surfer defies Galactus, protects Earth, and is once again left alone among humans who do not understand him. (Details of this out-of-continuity book are summarised here briefly; readers should consult the book itself for the full sequence of events.)"], "why": "The last major Lee–Kirby collaboration on the character and the only original story in the Fireside line; a bridge between their 1960s work and Kirby’s cosmic ideas of the 1970s.", "src": ["Wikipedia: Silver Surfer (comic book) – Volume 1/OGNs", "Marvel Database: Fireside Book Series Vol 1 11"]}, {"id": "ss-1982", "era": "Bronze Age (1970–1986)", "title": "Silver Surfer (vol. 2) one-shot", "sub": "Silver Surfer #1 (1982)", "img": "ss2_1", "gallery": [], "year": "1982", "dates": "June 1982 (cover date)", "creators": "John Byrne (plot, pencils), Stan Lee (script), Tom Palmer (inks)", "issues": "Silver Surfer vol. 2 #1 (one-shot)", "importance": 3, "syn": ["Reed Richards devises a way to get the Surfer past Galactus’s barrier – but only temporarily. Racing home, Norrin finds Zenn-La devastated: Galactus has drained its ecosystem in revenge for his herald’s betrayal, sparing its people only by warning them in advance. Shalla-Bal is missing, taken by Mephisto.", "Realising the truth behind Doom’s Latverian bride ‘Helena’, the Surfer chooses love over freedom and returns to Earth, re-trapping himself behind the barrier. He confronts Mephisto, rescues Shalla-Bal, and – unable to go with her – gives her a portion of his Power Cosmic before she is sent home. Shalla uses it to bring life back to Zenn-La’s barren surface, while Norrin remains alone on Earth, his sacrifice repeated."], "why": "Resolved the long-running Shalla-Bal/‘Helena’ mystery, showed the cost of his rebellion to his homeworld, and is fondly remembered for Byrne’s art. A panel from it later became the cover of Joe Satriani’s album Surfing with the Alien (1987).", "src": ["Wikipedia: Shalla-Bal; Surfing with the Alien", "Wikipedia: Silver Surfer (comic book) – Volume 2"]}, {"id": "secret-wars", "era": "Bronze Age (1970–1986)", "title": "Secret Wars II (1985–86) and Secret Wars (2015)", "sub": "Crossover appearances", "img": "sw2_1", "gallery": ["secw1"], "year": "1985 / 2015", "dates": "Secret Wars II: July 1985 – March 1986; Secret Wars: 2015–16", "creators": "Secret Wars II: Jim Shooter & Al Milgrom; Secret Wars (2015): Jonathan Hickman & Esad Ribić", "issues": "Secret Wars II #1–9 and tie-ins; Silver Surfer vol. 7 #14–15 tie into the 2015 Secret Wars", "importance": 2, "syn": ["A point worth clarifying: the Surfer was not one of the heroes taken to Battleworld in the original Secret Wars (1984–85), although Galactus was. He does appear in its sequel, Secret Wars II, in which the Beyonder comes to Earth in human form; the Surfer is among the cosmic-level heroes caught up in the Beyonder’s experiments with humanity.", "In 2015 the Multiverse ended in Jonathan Hickman’s Secret Wars. The Surfer’s own series (Slott/Allred, vol. 7 #14–15, ‘Last Days’) shows what happens to him and Dawn Greenwood as reality collapses – a story covered in the Slott/Allred entry – and its sequel (vol. 8) opens with the Surfer helping to restore the universe afterwards."], "why": "Included for completeness and to correct a common misconception; neither event is a central Surfer story.", "src": ["Marvel Database: Secret Wars II Vol 1 1; Secret Wars Vol 1 1", "Wikipedia: Silver Surfer (biography – Glorian/Shaper rebuild)"]}, {"id": "ss-vol3-englehart", "era": "Cosmic Renaissance (1987–1998)", "title": "Freedom: Silver Surfer vol. 3 – the Englehart era", "sub": "Silver Surfer vol. 3 #1–31, Annual #1–2 (1987–1989)", "img": "ss3_1", "gallery": ["ss3_15", "ss3_25", "ss3_31", "mfan51", "ss3_-1"], "year": "1987–1989", "dates": "July 1987 – December 1989 (cover dates)", "creators": "Steve Englehart (writer #1–31); Marshall Rogers (pencils, early issues); Ron Lim (pencils from #15 onward); Joe Rubinstein and others (inks)", "issues": "#1 escape from Earth; Elders of the Universe saga (early issues); Mantis storyline; Kree–Skrull war around Zenn-La; Annual #1 (Evolutionary War); Marvel Fanfare #51 (the shelved original #1)", "importance": 4, "syn": ["Englehart originally wrote a first issue still set on Earth; Marvel then agreed to lift the twenty-year exile, so that issue was shelved (it later saw print in Marvel Fanfare #51) and a new #1 (July 1987) was written to free him. After the Surfer’s latest failed attempt on the barrier, the Fantastic Four brainstorm with him and the Thing points out the obvious: the barrier is keyed to the board. The Surfer leaves Earth aboard a spacecraft, then summons the board after him. Galactus agrees to end the exile if the Surfer rescues his current herald Nova from the Skrulls, which he does.", "Free at last, Norrin races home – but Shalla-Bal, now empress of a revived Zenn-La, cannot simply resume their romance. Englehart then throws the Surfer into galactic politics. Zenn-La is caught between the Kree and the Skrulls in a renewed war, and the Elders of the Universe (the Champion, the Collector, the Grandmaster, the Contemplator, the Runner, the Possessor and others) scheme to destroy Galactus and remake the universe to their liking. The Surfer gathers allies, including Mantis, the former Avenger known as the Celestial Madonna, who becomes his companion and love interest; she appears to die in the Elders storyline. The run mixes space opera, philosophy and romance.", "Englehart wanted to build towards a story with Mistress Death and Thanos, but editorial reserved Thanos for a bigger event and restricted his use of Mantis. He left with #31, and after a few fill-ins Jim Starlin took over with #34 – picking up the very Thanos idea Englehart had been denied."], "why": "Ended the Earthbound era for good and reinvented the Surfer as Marvel’s premier cosmic hero, giving him a long-running ongoing series (146 issues, 1987–1998) and pairing him with Ron Lim, whose art defined the character for a generation.", "src": ["Wikipedia: Silver Surfer (publication history)", "Marvel Database: Silver Surfer Vol 3 1", "Wikipedia: The Infinity Gauntlet (background)"], "deep": ["Englehart’s run has three engines. First, freedom: the Surfer’s exile ends early, his relationship with Galactus is renegotiated, and he is free to go home to Zenn-La and Shalla-Bal – only to find that home is not what he remembers. Second, politics: he walks into the Kree–Skrull war, with Zenn-La drawn in, the Skrulls’ shape-shifting restored and the Kree manipulating Shalla-Bal’s court. Third, the Elders of the Universe – the Collector, the Grandmaster, the Runner, the Champion and their kin – whose plot against Galactus becomes a cosmic war in which the Surfer, granted clemency, fights beside his old master.", "Mantis, the former Avenger, joins the story as the Surfer’s lover, which is part of why Englehart’s plans collided with editorial: according to Wikipedia, he left after #31 because of restrictions over Mantis and Thanos, whose return Jim Starlin was about to handle. Marshall Rogers’s architectural pencils defined the first issues; Ron Lim’s clean, dynamic line took over from #15 and would define the next decade.", "The run’s importance is structural: it turned the Surfer from a guest star with an Earth-bound gimmick into a full-time cosmic protagonist, and set up the players – Thanos, Death, the Elders, Nova, the Kree and Skrulls – of the Infinity era."]}, {"id": "judgment-day", "era": "Cosmic Renaissance (1987–1998)", "title": "Silver Surfer: Judgment Day", "sub": "Marvel Graphic Novel #38 (1988)", "img": "mgn38", "gallery": [], "year": "1988", "dates": "Cover-dated October 1988 (on sale July 1988)", "creators": "Script credited by Marvel Database to Tom DeFalco (some sources credit Stan Lee); art by John Buscema", "issues": "Marvel Graphic Novel #38", "importance": 2, "syn": ["Mephisto again tries to claim the Surfer’s soul. After the Surfer resists a demonic trap, Mephisto corrupts Galactus’s herald Nova, pushing her to lead Galactus to worlds regardless of whether they are inhabited. The Surfer confronts her; Galactus, angered by his heralds’ quarrel, strands them both on the disputed planet.", "Desperate to stop Galactus, the Surfer agrees to give Mephisto his soul in exchange for escape – and Mephisto drags both heralds into his realm. Galactus follows, and the cosmic devourer and the lord of hell face off; Galactus threatens to consume Mephisto’s domain and forces him to release the two heralds. The story pairs the Surfer’s two great antagonists and is a showcase for Buscema’s return to the character."], "why": "A lavish, self-contained showdown between Galactus and Mephisto, often recommended alongside Parable as a stand-alone read. (Writer credit is reported inconsistently, so it is hedged here.)", "src": ["Marvel Database: Marvel Graphic Novel Vol 1 38", "Silver Surfer Epic Collection vol. 4 contents (Wikipedia)"]}, {"id": "parable", "era": "Cosmic Renaissance (1987–1998)", "title": "Silver Surfer: Parable", "sub": "Silver Surfer vol. 4 #1–2 (Epic Comics)", "img": "ss4_1", "gallery": ["parable"], "year": "1988–1989", "dates": "December 1988 – January 1989 (two issues)", "creators": "Stan Lee (story), Mœbius / Jean Giraud (art, partial colours and lettering)", "issues": "Silver Surfer vol. 4 #1–2; collected as Silver Surfer: Parable (Eisner Award, Best Finite Series 1989)", "importance": 5, "syn": ["Mœbius wanted to try an American comic; Lee handed him a short plot outline within days of meeting him, and Mœbius drew it using the ‘Marvel method’. The result is a fable that sits apart from regular continuity. Galactus’s ship crashes into a great city and the devourer announces himself as humanity’s new god. Because he once vowed not to harm Earth, he does not attack it – instead he declares that there are no more laws, sins or limits, and lets humanity tear itself apart.", "The Surfer has been living as a silent vagrant, his board wrapped in rags. A fading televangelist, Colton Candell, appoints himself Galactus’s prophet and grows rich and powerful as society collapses into anarchy. His sister Elyna is drawn to the stranger who dares to oppose Galactus. The Surfer challenges his former master, who answers that he is keeping his word – humans are simply destroying themselves. When the Surfer tries to warn the crowds, they turn on him for blasphemy.", "In the second issue Galactus and the Surfer battle across the city. Elyna, realising her mistake, tries to intervene and is fatally shot by one of Candell’s men; the Surfer breaks off the fight to save her and is struck down. Her death, broadcast to the world, turns humanity against Galactus, who withdraws. The world’s leaders then offer to make the Surfer their ruler and object of worship. Horrified that people only want another god, he deliberately plays the tyrant until they reject him, and departs – alone again, understood by almost no one."], "why": "One of the most celebrated Surfer stories and a rare collaboration between a Marvel founder and a European master. Its themes – blind faith, demagoguery, the refusal of worship – distil Lee’s view of the character as a Christ-like outsider.", "src": ["Wikipedia: Silver Surfer: Parable", "Wikipedia: Silver Surfer (comic book) – Awards"], "deep": ["Parable was published in 1988–89 through Marvel’s Epic Comics imprint as two oversized issues, officially Silver Surfer vol. 4. Lee supplied a short story; Mœbius drew it from his outline in the Marvel method, so the visual pacing is his – sparse, airy, with long silent sequences that look nothing like Marvel house style. The project grew out of Lee’s admiration for European comics and was part of the late-1980s boom in prestige formats.", "The book’s theme is religion and authority. Galactus, bound by an old promise not to consume Earth, returns anyway and lets humanity worship him; a cult rises around the giant, and the Surfer must decide whether to break his own self-imposed rule of non-interference. The Surfer’s final position – that humanity must be free even to make mistakes, and must not bow to any god, including him – is the clearest statement of Lee’s view of the character.", "It won the 1989 Eisner Award for Best Finite Series/Limited Series and has been reprinted many times, including in the Epic Collection volume that bears its name. Its reputation outside comics is helped by pop-culture references, most famously the Crimson Tide argument comparing Kirby’s Surfer with Mœbius’s."]}, {"id": "rebirth-of-thanos", "era": "Cosmic Renaissance (1987–1998)", "title": "The Rebirth of Thanos", "sub": "Silver Surfer vol. 3 #34–38 (1990)", "img": "ss3_34", "gallery": ["ss3_38", "ss3_44", "ss3_48"], "year": "1990", "dates": "February – June 1990 (cover dates; #34 on sale Dec 1989)", "creators": "Jim Starlin (writer), Ron Lim (pencils)", "issues": "Silver Surfer vol. 3 #34–38 (collected in Silver Surfer: Rebirth of Thanos with The Thanos Quest)", "importance": 5, "syn": ["Jim Starlin’s first issue as writer opens with the resurrection of Thanos, the Mad Titan, who had been dead since Starlin’s 1970s stories. Mistress Death – the cosmic embodiment Thanos loves – has brought him back because she feels the universe holds more living beings than dead ones, and she wants the balance corrected. Thanos sets about the task with grim enthusiasm.", "The Surfer senses the threat and becomes Thanos’s main obstacle. Starlin also brings back Drax the Destroyer, the man created solely to kill Thanos, now with a damaged mind. In a pattern Starlin loved, Thanos manipulates the Surfer and others, and at the end of the arc fakes his own death so that no one will interfere with the far bigger scheme he is now contemplating – the gathering of the six Infinity Gems.", "The story leads directly into The Thanos Quest (1990) and, through further Silver Surfer issues, into The Infinity Gauntlet (1991)."], "why": "The opening act of Starlin’s ‘Infinity’ saga – Marvel’s most influential cosmic storyline, later loosely adapted for the films Avengers: Infinity War and Endgame – and the run that made the Surfer a top seller.", "src": ["Wikipedia: The Infinity Gauntlet (background)", "Wikipedia: The Thanos Quest", "Marvel Database: Silver Surfer Vol 3 34"], "deep": ["Jim Starlin took over Silver Surfer vol. 3 from #34 (1990) and immediately brought back Thanos, whom Death had resurrected to restore the balance between the living and the dead by killing half of all life. Thanos’s scheming runs alongside a deeper story about Norrin himself.", "In #48–50, with Ron Lim on art, Starlin explains a nagging question: why did the noble Norrin Radd lead Galactus to inhabited worlds for so long? The answer is that Galactus altered his soul, dulling his conscience. Restored, Norrin is confronted with the full guilt of his herald years, including memories of his parents’ suicides (Jartran and Elmar) and the scale of the deaths he enabled. #50 – a landmark anniversary issue – is also where he comes to terms with it.", "The arc (collected as Rebirth of Thanos and in the Return of Thanos/Thanos Quest Epic Collections) leads straight into Thanos Quest and The Infinity Gauntlet; it is the moment the Surfer became the moral centre of Marvel’s biggest cosmic saga."]}, {"id": "thanos-quest", "era": "Cosmic Renaissance (1987–1998)", "title": "The Thanos Quest", "sub": "Thanos Quest #1–2 (1990)", "img": "tq1", "gallery": [], "year": "1990", "dates": "September – October 1990", "creators": "Jim Starlin (writer), Ron Lim (pencils)", "issues": "Thanos Quest #1–2 (spun out of Silver Surfer)", "importance": 3, "syn": ["Thanos learns from Death’s Infinity Well the true nature of the six ‘Soul Gems’: they are Infinity Gems, fragments of a primordial being, each governing an aspect of existence. With Death’s permission he sets out to take them from the cosmic beings who hold them. He frees the In-Betweener only to seize the Soul Gem; tricks the Champion into a deal that costs him the Power Gem; overcomes the Gardener (Time Gem); ages the Runner into an infant (Space Gem) and trades the child to the Collector for the Reality Gem; and finally outwits the Grandmaster in a lethal game for the Mind Gem.", "He returns to Death a near-omnipotent being – and she will still only speak to him through servants, explaining that he is now her superior rather than her equal. Thanos leaves in a rage, wondering why godhood feels so hollow. The Surfer is not in this miniseries, but it is the hinge between his own book and the Infinity Gauntlet."], "why": "Essential context for the Surfer’s central role in The Infinity Gauntlet; a compact, much-reprinted Thanos story.", "src": ["Wikipedia: The Thanos Quest", "Marvel Database: Thanos Quest Vol 1 1"]}, {"id": "infinity-gauntlet", "era": "Cosmic Renaissance (1987–1998)", "title": "The Infinity Gauntlet", "sub": "The Infinity Gauntlet #1–6 (1991)", "img": "ig4", "gallery": ["ss3_44", "ss3_50", "ss3_51", "ss3_50"], "year": "1991", "dates": "July – December 1991; lead-in Silver Surfer vol. 3 #44–50", "creators": "Jim Starlin (writer); George Pérez (pencils #1–3 and part of #4), Ron Lim (pencils #4–6)", "issues": "The Infinity Gauntlet #1–6; lead-ins Silver Surfer vol. 3 #44–50; tie-ins in Silver Surfer #51–59 and other titles", "importance": 5, "syn": ["In the Silver Surfer issues leading up to the event (#44–50), the Surfer and Drax try – and fail – to stop Thanos assembling the Infinity Gauntlet. The Surfer learns something painful along the way: when he became Galactus’s herald, Galactus had altered his soul so that he could tolerate leading his master to inhabited worlds. Galactus restores it, and the Surfer is suddenly overwhelmed by guilt for the billions who died on his watch, until he finds a way to forgive himself.", "The Infinity Gauntlet #1 opens with Thanos, now omnipotent, trying to impress Death. He creates a consort, Terraxia, and, to win Death’s favour, snaps his fingers and erases half of all life in the universe. Before the cosmic chaos fully hits, the Surfer crashes through the roof of Doctor Strange’s Sanctum Sanctorum on Earth to warn of what is coming. Adam Warlock, newly returned from the Soul Gem, takes charge, and Earth’s surviving heroes are assembled for an assault on Thanos while cosmic entities – Galactus, Eternity, Lord Chaos and Master Order, the Stranger and others – mount their own attack.", "The battle is a massacre: Thanos kills or neutralises the heroes one by one, though the Surfer comes within a hair of snatching the Gauntlet at a key moment. Thanos then defeats Eternity itself and takes its place, leaving his body behind. His granddaughter Nebula seizes the Gauntlet from the empty body and undoes Thanos’s acts, restoring the dead; Warlock, in turn, takes the Gauntlet from her. Thanos survives and, in a rare turn, retires to a quiet life as a farmer – while the Living Tribunal rules that the Gems can no longer be used together and Warlock forms the Infinity Watch to guard them."], "why": "Marvel’s defining cosmic event of the 1990s, with the Surfer as the herald of the crisis and one of its key combatants. It sold enormously, cemented the Surfer–Thanos rivalry and later inspired the MCU’s Infinity Saga.", "src": ["Wikipedia: The Infinity Gauntlet", "Wikipedia: Silver Surfer (biography – soul restored)", "Marvel Database: Infinity Gauntlet Vol 1 1–6"], "deep": ["The Surfer’s role in The Infinity Gauntlet (1991) is that of herald in reverse: he is the one who sees Thanos’s power for what it is and crashes into Doctor Strange’s sanctum to warn Earth. He then fights in the doomed assault on Thanos, survives the massacre of heroes, and plays a part in the final reversal when Nebula takes the Gauntlet and Adam Warlock recovers it.", "George Pérez pencilled #1–3 and part of #4; Ron Lim completed the series. Starlin designed the event so that the Silver Surfer book fed directly into it, and the tie-ins in Silver Surfer vol. 3 #50–60 or so carried the story forward. Sales were huge, and the Surfer’s presence on covers and in the main narrative made his title one of Marvel’s best sellers of the early 1990s.", "The event’s legacy shaped the MCU’s Avengers: Infinity War and Endgame, though the Surfer – then tied to Fox’s Fantastic Four rights – does not appear in those films."]}, {"id": "infinity-war-crusade", "era": "Cosmic Renaissance (1987–1998)", "title": "The Infinity War and The Infinity Crusade", "sub": "Infinity War #1–6 (1992); Infinity Crusade #1–6 (1993)", "img": "iw1", "gallery": ["ic1"], "year": "1992–1993", "dates": "Infinity War: June – November 1992; Infinity Crusade: June – November 1993", "creators": "Jim Starlin (writer), Ron Lim (pencils), with tie-ins across the line", "issues": "Infinity War #1–6 and tie-ins; Infinity Crusade #1–6 and tie-ins (Silver Surfer vol. 3 #83–85 tie into Crusade)", "importance": 3, "syn": ["Infinity War: When Adam Warlock purged himself of good and evil to become a ‘perfect’ wielder of the Gauntlet, those aspects took on lives of their own. The evil one, the Magus, returns with an army of doppelgängers of Earth’s heroes and schemes to obtain the Gauntlet. Warlock forges an uneasy alliance with Thanos, and the Surfer is among the cosmic heroes drawn into the conflict as duplicates infiltrate the hero community. The Magus is eventually defeated after a battle across the cosmos.", "Infinity Crusade: Warlock’s good aspect, the Goddess, gathers the Cosmic Cubes to create a ‘perfect’ universe and uses a kind of religious mind control to recruit Earth’s most spiritually inclined heroes – including, at first, the Silver Surfer – to her crusade on her world Paradise Omega. The Surfer later throws off her influence. Guided by information from Mephisto, Thanos and Warlock lead the remaining heroes; the Surfer smashes Paradise Omega’s defences so they can land, and Thanos finally confronts the Goddess as she tries to impose her vision."], "why": "Completed Starlin’s Infinity trilogy. The Surfer is a supporting rather than central player, but the books are part of his most popular era.", "src": ["Wikipedia: The Infinity Crusade", "Marvel Database: Infinity War Vol 1 1; Infinity Crusade Vol 1 1"]}, {"id": "ss-vol3-marz", "era": "Cosmic Renaissance (1987–1998)", "title": "Silver Surfer vol. 3 highlights: Marz, Herald Ordeal and the end of an era", "sub": "Silver Surfer vol. 3 #51–146 (1991–1998)", "img": "ss3_75", "gallery": ["ss3_70", "ss3_100", "ss3_123", "ss3_146", "ens", "ss3_60", "ss3_69", "ss3_81", "ss3_100b", "ss3_122", "ss3_130"], "year": "1991–1998", "dates": "July 1991 – November 1998", "creators": "Ron Marz (writer, from #51 into the mid-#100s), later J. M. DeMatteis, George Pérez and Glenn Greenberg; artists Ron Lim, M. C. Wyman, Tom Grindberg, Joe Phillips, Ron Garney, Jon J. Muth and others", "issues": "#51–59 (Infinity Gauntlet tie-ins), #70–75 ‘Herald Ordeal’ (Morg; #75 death of Nova, Dec 1992), #100 (1995), #123 onward (DeMatteis), #146 final issue (Nov 1998)", "importance": 4, "syn": ["Ron Marz succeeded Starlin and carried the book through the Infinity tie-ins and beyond. His signature arc, ‘The Herald Ordeal’ (#70–75, 1992), turns on the Surfer’s feelings for Nova (Frankie Raye), Galactus’s current herald. The Surfer’s influence makes Nova question her role, and Galactus replaces her with the far more ruthless Morg, a merciless executioner. A band of current and former heralds clashes with Morg, and in #75 (‘Destruction’, Dec 1992) Nova is killed. The Surfer is left devastated.", "Later stories send him back in time, into conflict with the bounty hunter Raze and other cosmic menaces, and into the Starlin-led spin-offs (Warlock and the Infinity Watch, Cosmic Powers, Silver Surfer/Warlock: Resurrection). In the later issues of the run (mid-to-late 1990s, including J. M. DeMatteis’s tenure from #123) the tone turns introspective. The Surfer discovers that Zenn-La has vanished – and learns that the real world was destroyed long ago (in the 1940s of Earth time) by an entity called the Other. The Zenn-La and the people he had been visiting since his freedom were reproductions made by Galactus so that his former herald would have a home to return to. Losing his capacity for emotion, he returns to Earth; a time-travel adventure restores his personality and he begins a romance with Alicia Masters, the woman who first reached him in 1966. They eventually part as friends. At one point in this period he is also briefly bonded to, and controlled by, the Carnage symbiote.", "The series ended with #146 (Nov 1998), written by Glenn Greenberg. In its 1987–1998 run it produced 146 issues, a #−1 and annuals, making it by far the longest Surfer series; the 1990s also produced original graphic novels such as The Enslavers (1990) and Homecoming (1991)."], "why": "The longest and most commercially successful Surfer series. Its peak years spun off a 1990 video game, trading-card sets and the 1998 animated series.", "src": ["Wikipedia: Silver Surfer (comic book & biography)", "Marvel Database: Silver Surfer Vol 3 75, 146"], "deep": ["Ron Marz’s run, from #51 into the #100s, is the core of the 1990s Surfer. Its centrepiece is ‘The Herald Ordeal’ (1992): Galactus, tired of heralds with consciences, chooses Morg the Executioner to replace Nova. The ex-heralds – the Surfer, Firelord, Terrax and the rebuilt Air-Walker – band together, and Morg kills Nova in #75, a death the Surfer never quite gets over.", "The Tyrant saga (beginning #81, 1993) reveals that Galactus once built a companion who became a galaxy-burning conqueror. Later issues include the Surfer’s reunion with Zenn-La and the discovery of his half-brother Fennan with Shalla-Bal (#100–101), a romance with Alicia Masters, and in the late #120s–130s, under J. M. DeMatteis, a journey through time in which he learns that Zenn-La had been destroyed in 1947 by the Other and recreated by Galactus (#130–131).", "The run’s artists – Lim, M. C. Wyman, Tom Grindberg, Ron Garney, Jon J. Muth and others – and its writers kept the book monthly through Marvel’s 1990s boom and bust; vol. 3 ran 146 issues (plus #−1 and nine annuals) from 1987 to the end of the decade – the longest-running Surfer series."]}, {"id": "ss-vol5", "era": "Modern Age (1999–2012)", "title": "Silver Surfer vol. 5: Communion & Revelation", "sub": "Silver Surfer vol. 5 #1–14 (2003–2004)", "img": "ss5_1", "gallery": [], "year": "2003–2004", "dates": "September 2003 – December 2004 (cover dates)", "creators": "Dan Chariton & Stacy Weiss (writers); artists including Milx and Lan Medina", "issues": "#1–6 ‘Communion’, #7–14 ‘Revelation’", "importance": 2, "syn": ["Note on numbering: this 2003 series is officially volume 5 (the 1982 one-shot is vol. 2 and Parable is vol. 4), not volume 4, and it is not by Dan Slott and Mike Allred – their run came in 2014. The writing team of Dan Chariton and Stacy Weiss leaned into the Surfer’s alien nature and a messianic allegory.", "The Surfer returns to Earth and works with the alien Annunaki to find and protect a group of extraordinarily gifted human children, who are being hunted as a looming apocalyptic threat approaches. The threat takes the shape of the godlike entity Marduk. In the climax one of the children, Ellie Waters, saves the Earth and reorders reality so that the Marduk crisis never happened – with Ellie apparently the only one left remembering it. The Surfer resumes his wanderings, promising to return if his adopted world needs him."], "why": "A short-lived, divisive attempt to reposition the Surfer as a spiritual, almost religious figure; included for completeness of the solo series.", "src": ["Wikipedia: Silver Surfer (comic book) – Volume 5; Silver Surfer (biography)", "Marvel Database: Silver Surfer Vol 5 1"]}, {"id": "planet-hulk", "era": "Modern Age (1999–2012)", "title": "Planet Hulk and Skaar", "sub": "Incredible Hulk vol. 2 #95 (2006); Skaar: Son of Hulk (2008)", "img": "hulk95", "gallery": ["ph1", "skaar1", "skaar7"], "year": "2006–2008", "dates": "Incredible Hulk #95: July 2006; Skaar: Son of Hulk from August 2008", "creators": "Greg Pak (writer); Carlo Pagulayan (Planet Hulk pencils); Ron Garney (Skaar)", "issues": "Incredible Hulk vol. 2 #95 (part of ‘Planet Hulk’, #92–105); Skaar: Son of Hulk #1–12", "importance": 2, "syn": ["Planet Hulk: a portal from the brutal empire of Sakaar snatches the Surfer, leaving him drained. Subdued and fitted with an obedience disk, he is forced to fight as a gladiator – where some locals hail him as the prophesied ‘Sakaarson’ because of his appearance – and is pitted against the Hulk and his Warbound. Through teamwork and distraction the Hulk destroys the disk. The freed Surfer uses the Power Cosmic to remove the other slaves’ disks and blasts open the arena, then offers to take the Hulk back to Earth. The Hulk – exiled by Earth’s heroes – refuses.", "Skaar: years later, the Surfer, now once again a herald, returns to Sakaar intending to feed Galactus with its unique ‘Old Power’, which he believes would sate his master for millennia and spare countless other worlds. The Hulk’s son Skaar opposes him, and the Surfer is again enslaved by an obedience disk. The crisis ends when Skaar’s mother, Caiera, gives up her soul and Old Power as food for Galactus – leaving Galactus hungry for more Old Power."], "why": "A memorable guest role in one of the most acclaimed Hulk stories (adapted loosely in the film Thor: Ragnarok, which did not use the Surfer).", "src": ["Wikipedia: Silver Surfer (biography)", "Marvel Database: Incredible Hulk Vol 2 95"]}, {"id": "annihilation", "era": "Modern Age (1999–2012)", "title": "Annihilation, Annihilation: Silver Surfer and Heralds of Galactus", "sub": "Annihilation: Silver Surfer #1–4 (2006); Annihilation #1–6 (2006–07); Annihilation: Heralds of Galactus #1–2 (2007)", "img": "annss1", "gallery": ["ann1", "ann6", "her1", "annih_1"], "year": "2006–2007", "dates": "Annihilation: Silver Surfer: June – Sept 2006 (cover; #1 on sale April 2006); Annihilation #1–6: Oct 2006 – Mar 2007; Heralds of Galactus: April – May 2007", "creators": "Keith Giffen (writer, Annihilation and Annihilation: Silver Surfer); Renato Arlem (art, Annihilation: Silver Surfer); Andrea Di Vito (art, Annihilation); Christos N. Gage and others (Heralds of Galactus)", "issues": "Annihilation: Prologue; Annihilation: Silver Surfer #1–4; Annihilation #1–6; Annihilation: Heralds of Galactus #1–2", "importance": 4, "syn": ["Annihilus, ruler of the Negative Zone, breaks into our universe with the Annihilation Wave, an insect-like armada that destroys the Nova Corps and sweeps across Kree and Skrull space. In Annihilation: Silver Surfer the Surfer investigates and learns that Annihilus has dispatched hunters, the Seekers led by Ravenous, to capture beings who carry the Power Cosmic. Teaming with his fellow ex-heralds Firelord and Air-Walker, he fights back; Air-Walker is killed and Terrax and Morg are captured. To protect Galactus, the Surfer does what he swore he never would: he takes Stardust’s place and becomes Galactus’s herald once more.", "In the main Annihilation series, Richard Rider (Nova) builds the United Front – Drax, Gamora, Ronan, Star-Lord and others – while Firelord, Red Shift and Stardust lead the space forces. It emerges that Thanos, allied to Annihilus, had helped the ancient beings Tenebrous and Aegis bring down Galactus and the Surfer, whom Annihilus plans to use as a weapon. Thanos decides to betray Annihilus, but Drax kills him first. Drax and Moondragon free the Surfer; the Surfer frees Galactus. An enraged Galactus unleashes an enormous blast – the ‘Galactus event’ – that wipes out most of the Annihilation Wave, with the Surfer flying ahead of it to warn those in its path. Nova then kills Annihilus.", "In Heralds of Galactus, Galactus sends the Surfer after Tenebrous and Aegis, and the Surfer destroys them using the energy of the Crunch, the boundary at the edge of the universe."], "why": "Relaunched Marvel’s cosmic line in the 2000s, leading to Annihilation: Conquest and the modern Guardians of the Galaxy; it put the Surfer back at Galactus’s side for several years.", "src": ["Wikipedia: Annihilation (comics)", "Marvel Database: Annihilation: Silver Surfer Vol 1 1; Annihilation: Heralds of Galactus Vol 1 1"], "deep": ["Annihilation (2006) began when Annihilus’s Annihilation Wave burst out of the Negative Zone and destroyed the Nova Corps. In the four-issue Annihilation: Silver Surfer, the Surfer learns that Galactus is the key to stopping it and – in a reversal of his whole history – returns to serve as his herald to gain the power needed. Air-Walker dies, which is part of what persuades him.", "Galactus and the Surfer are defeated by Tenebrous and Aegis and delivered to Annihilus (with Thanos’s connivance), and Galactus is used as a weapon; Drax frees him, and Galactus retaliates by destroying most of Annihilus’s forces. Galactus then sends the Surfer to find the Proemial Gods Tenebrous and Aegis – freed when Annihilus destroyed the Kyln – for a final confrontation.", "The event made the Surfer a herald again for the rest of the decade and gave Marvel’s cosmic line a new spine, leading into the Guardians of the Galaxy (2008) revival."]}, {"id": "requiem", "era": "Modern Age (1999–2012)", "title": "Silver Surfer: Requiem", "sub": "Silver Surfer: Requiem #1–4 (Marvel Knights, 2007)", "img": "req1", "gallery": ["req4"], "year": "2007", "dates": "July – October 2007 (cover); #1 released 30/31 May 2007 to coincide with the film", "creators": "J. Michael Straczynski (writer), Esad Ribić (painted art)", "issues": "Silver Surfer: Requiem #1–4 (set in an alternate reality, Earth-77119 in Marvel Database terms)", "importance": 4, "syn": ["Told outside main continuity, Requiem imagines the Surfer’s last days. He learns that the silver shell that protects him is deteriorating and that he is dying. He comes to Earth, where Reed Richards confirms that nothing can be done, and he decides how to spend the time he has left.", "Across the four painted issues the Surfer revisits the people and places that shaped him – spending time with Earth’s heroes (Spider-Man, Doctor Strange and the Fantastic Four appear) and using his remaining power to make a lasting difference on a distant world locked in conflict. The final issue, ‘Agnus Dei’, takes him home to Zenn-La and to Shalla-Bal, where he spends his final moments; Galactus and the Watcher are present at the end, giving the story the feel of a cosmic funeral rite. (This summary is intentionally broad to avoid misstating specific scenes.)"], "why": "A widely praised, elegiac ‘last story’ for the character, released alongside his first movie appearance.", "src": ["Wikipedia: Silver Surfer (2000s)", "Marvel Database: Silver Surfer: Requiem Vol 1 1, 4"], "deep": ["Issue #2 is the most quoted: in New York the Surfer saves Spider-Man from a criminal in a stolen military suit, then asks him what he should do with the time and power he has left. Every idea – ending poverty, ending war – would only last as long as he did. Spider-Man’s eventual answer leads the Surfer to spread the Power Cosmic across the whole Earth, giving everyone five minutes of true peace; any longer, he explains, could kill him. He also lets Mary Jane ride his board through space.", "Later issues take him to a distant world at war, where he uses his remaining power to force a lasting change, and finally home to Zenn-La. Ribić’s painted pages, released alongside the 2007 film, made Requiem one of the most admired Surfer books of the century, though it remains outside regular continuity."]}, {"id": "in-thy-name", "era": "Modern Age (1999–2012)", "title": "Silver Surfer: In Thy Name", "sub": "Silver Surfer: In Thy Name #1–4 (2008)", "img": "itn1", "gallery": [], "year": "2007–2008", "dates": "January – April 2008 (cover; #1 released Nov 2007)", "creators": "Simon Spurrier (writer), Tan Eng Huat (art)", "issues": "#1–4", "importance": 1, "syn": ["Spurrier and Tan Eng Huat’s four-issue miniseries (2008) sends the Surfer to the Ama Collective, a technologically advanced, apparently perfect empire whose ruling elite – including the Empress and the aristocrat Ruqtar Koil – has abolished religion as irrational. Beyond its borders live the Brekk, an ‘ancient enemy’ species, on the world Brekknis.", "The Surfer’s attempt to bring peace goes wrong in a way only he could cause: the Brekk start to worship him, and the Ama exploit it. According to Marvel Database and contemporary reviews, the Surfer suffers a ‘phase-crucifixion’ on Brekknis, the Ama aristocracy manipulates events to create a pretext for war, and – trying to scare both sides into peace – the Surfer projects a vast astral image of Galactus, a bluff that fails. Koil dies in the finale (#4), and the series ends with the Surfer facing the limits of the Power Cosmic: it can threaten, but it cannot make two peoples stop hating each other.", "It is a deliberately odd, satirical book – organ-pirates, astral squid-beasts, conniving aristocrats – and a pointed commentary on how quickly a ‘rational’ utopia can turn a saviour into a political weapon."], "why": "A sharp, underrated satire on utopias, worship and interference – and on the Surfer’s own capacity to make things worse.", "src": ["Wikipedia: Silver Surfer (2000s)", "Marvel Database: Silver Surfer: In Thy Name Vol 1 1"], "deep": []}, {"id": "cosmic-2010", "era": "Modern Age (1999–2012)", "title": "Cosmic teams and Asgard: Thanos Imperative, Annihilators, Devolution, Mighty Thor and Defenders", "sub": "2010–2012", "img": "mthor1", "gallery": ["ti1", "annih1", "ss6_1", "def4_1", "tim1", "chaos2", "fiu1", "mthor2"], "year": "2010–2012", "dates": "The Thanos Imperative (2010); Silver Surfer vol. 6 #1–5 (Apr–Aug 2011); Annihilators (2011); The Mighty Thor (from 2011); Defenders vol. 4 (2012)", "creators": "Dan Abnett & Andy Lanning (Thanos Imperative, Annihilators); Greg Pak with Stephen Segovia & Harvey Tolibao (vol. 6); Matt Fraction with Olivier Coipel (Mighty Thor) and Terry Dodson (Defenders)", "issues": "The Thanos Imperative #1–6; Annihilators #1–4; Silver Surfer vol. 6 #1–5 ‘Devolution’; The Mighty Thor #1–6 ‘The Galactus Seed’; Defenders vol. 4 #1–12; Fear Itself: The Deep", "importance": 2, "syn": ["After the Cancerverse invasion in The Thanos Imperative, the Surfer joined the Annihilators, a cosmic ‘super-team’ of heavy hitters such as Beta Ray Bill, Gladiator, Ronan, Quasar and Ikon.", "In his sixth solo volume, Greg Pak’s five-issue Devolution (2011), the High Evolutionary targets the Surfer as part of a scheme involving humanity’s evolution, separating Norrin Radd’s humanity from the Surfer’s power; the Future Foundation and the young Suzi Endo (the Seeker) are drawn in, and Galactus becomes part of the endgame.", "In Matt Fraction’s The Mighty Thor (2011), Galactus and the Surfer come to Earth for the Asgardian World Tree seed. The battle with Thor ends when the Surfer chooses to leave his post and guard the seed. Galactus ‘tethers’ him to Asgard (then in Broxton, Oklahoma), so his powers weaken the further he travels from it, and grants him the ability to return to human form. Fraction then made him a core member of a new Defenders (2012), and he aided Doctor Strange, Namor and others in Fear Itself: The Deep (2011)."], "why": "Kept the Surfer central to Marvel’s cosmic and Asgardian storylines and restored his ability to live as Norrin Radd.", "src": ["Wikipedia: Silver Surfer (2010s; biography)", "Marvel Database: Mighty Thor Vol 1 1; Silver Surfer Vol 6 1, 5"]}, {"id": "slott-allred", "era": "Slott & Allred and after (2014–2026)", "title": "Silver Surfer by Dan Slott & Mike Allred", "sub": "Silver Surfer vol. 7 #1–15 (2014–2015) and vol. 8 #1–14 (2016–2017)", "img": "ss7_1", "gallery": ["ss7_3", "ss7_11", "ss7_15", "ss8_1", "ss8_7", "ss8_14", "ss7_10", "ss8_13"], "year": "2014–2017", "dates": "Vol. 7: May 2014 – January 2016; vol. 8: March 2016 – December 2017 (cover dates)", "creators": "Dan Slott (writer); Michael Allred (pencils & inks); Laura Allred (colours)", "issues": "Vol. 7 #1–15 (New Dawn; Worlds Apart; Last Days); vol. 8 #1–14 (Citizen of Earth; A Power Greater Than Cosmic); #11 ‘Never After’ won the 2016 Eisner for Best Single Issue", "importance": 5, "syn": ["Slott and the Allreds pitched the series as ‘Doctor Who meets the Silver Surfer’. In vol. 7 #1 the Incredulous Zed of the Impericon, a pleasure planet, wants the Surfer as his champion against the Queen of Nevers. To force his hand, Zed uses a machine to identify the most important person in the Surfer’s life and kidnaps her – Dawn Greenwood, a young woman from Anchor Bay, Massachusetts, whom the Surfer has never met. Dawn is curious, stubborn and resolutely Earth-bound in a way the Surfer is not. Learning that the Impericon is powered by the Queen’s stolen heart, the Surfer and Dawn return it. Afterwards he offers to show her the universe; she names his board ‘Toomie’ (from his catchphrase ‘To me, my board!’).", "Their travels mix whimsy and guilt. On the hidden world of Newhaven they meet billions of refugees whose planets Galactus consumed, and Dawn learns about the Surfer’s past for the first time; she is horrified and asks to stay behind. Galactus, following the Surfer’s trail, then arrives at Newhaven, forcing the Surfer to make amends rather than run. Issue #11, ‘Never After’, is a formal tour de force built around a Möbius-strip-like story structure, and it won an Eisner Award. In #14–15 (‘Last Days’), the Multiverse ends in Secret Wars; the Surfer tries to save Dawn, Earth and everyone he can as reality dies.", "Vol. 8 opens after the universe is rebuilt: the Surfer helped remake the cosmos and, with Dawn, explores and settles into Earth life (‘Citizen of Earth’). Shalla-Bal returns as Keeper of the Great Truth, spreading Zenn-La’s culture across the universe – including onto Earth – and the Surfer has to undo her campaign by erasing Zenn-La’s culture so completely that even its own people cannot recall it; in gratitude the nations of Earth grant him citizenship. The final arc, ‘A Power Greater Than Cosmic’ (#14, Dec 2017), gives Norrin and Dawn’s relationship a bittersweet, time-spanning conclusion."], "why": "The most acclaimed modern run: funny, warm and inventive, with Dawn Greenwood becoming one of the Surfer’s most important companions. It brought in new readers who had never touched the 1990s cosmic books.", "src": ["Wikipedia: Silver Surfer (comic book) – vols 7–8; Awards", "Wikipedia: Silver Surfer (biography)", "Marvel Database: Norrin Radd (Earth-616) ‘Girl on Board’"], "deep": ["Volume 7 (2014–15) opens with the Surfer captured by the Incredulous Zed, ruler of the pleasure planet Impericon, who wants him as its protector and kidnaps Dawn Greenwood of Anchor Bay, Massachusetts, as leverage. The run then becomes a road trip through strange worlds, with Dawn and the board Toomie as the Surfer’s companions. #11, ‘Never After’ – a Möbius-strip issue that can be read in a loop – won the 2016 Eisner for Best Single Issue.", "Volume 8 (2016–17) brings him home. Shalla-Bal returns as the Keeper of the Great Truth, using Zenn-La’s Illuminatrix to remake worlds, including Earth; the Surfer stops her at the cost of erasing Zenn-La’s culture from the universe, and Earth’s nations offer him citizenship. After the death of Dawn’s father, a trip into the past overshoots into the previous universe, where (per Marvel Database) Norrin and Dawn marry and live a full life. The final issue sees Norrin return to the present, alone, having lost Dawn to age – one of the most moving endings in the character’s history.", "Slott pitched the series as ‘Doctor Who meets the Silver Surfer’, and the Allreds’ pop-art look matched the tone. The run is collected in five trades and an Omnibus (2018)."]}, {"id": "black", "era": "Slott & Allred and after (2014–2026)", "title": "Silver Surfer: Black", "sub": "Silver Surfer: Black #1–5 (2019)", "img": "ssb1", "gallery": ["gotg6_1", "ssb5"], "year": "2019", "dates": "August – December 2019 (cover; #1 released 12 June 2019)", "creators": "Donny Cates (writer), Tradd Moore (art), Dave Stewart (colours)", "issues": "Silver Surfer: Black #1–5; prologue in Guardians of the Galaxy vol. 6 #1–2 (2019)", "importance": 4, "syn": ["In Guardians of the Galaxy (2019) #1, Starfox gathers the cosmic heroes to hear Thanos’s posthumous will – and the Black Order attacks to steal Thanos’s body, creating a black hole as cover. To save the others, the Surfer tears open space-time and is pulled into the singularity himself.", "He emerges billions of years in the past, in the primordial dark before the stars were fully kindled, during the campaign of Knull, the god of the symbiotes. The Surfer fights Knull and is infected by a symbiote, the darkness spreading over his silver form (hence the title). Badly hurt, he is aided by Ego the Living Planet, gathers what light exists in the young cosmos and drives Knull back. In doing so he becomes part of the deep history that later stories (notably King in Black) build upon. He eventually finds his way back to his own era, changed by the experience.", "Tradd Moore’s fluid, psychedelic art (later also collected in an oversized treasury edition) made the book one of the most acclaimed Surfer stories of the 2010s."], "why": "A strong reinvention of the Surfer as a myth-scale bringer of light, and the direct set-up for his role in King in Black.", "src": ["Wikipedia: Silver Surfer (2010s)", "Marvel Database: Silver Surfer: Black Vol 1 1; Guardians of the Galaxy Vol 6 1"], "deep": ["Issue #3 opens with a nightmare: the Surfer dreams of his last morning with Shalla-Bal – in this version his first wife – and of a titanic, black-skinned version of himself devouring Zenn-La. He wakes orbiting Ego the Living Planet, his body suffering ‘rapid atomic degeneration’ after spending so much power creating stars in the young universe. Ego, still maturing, becomes his ally.", "In #5 the Surfer implants the light given to him by the cosmos into his chest and attacks Knull, who rides a symbiote dragon and wields the blade All-Black. His board becomes a sword; Knull bonds a symbiote to him; and Norrin spends the last of his Power Cosmic to create a star that blasts Knull across the cosmos. He knows it only slows the King in Black. Marvel Database adds that the time-displaced Surfer gave life to the world that would become Zenn-La – closing the loop of his own origin – before finding his way home.", "Black set up King in Black (2020–21), changed the Surfer’s look and powers, and is widely regarded as one of the best Surfer stories of the 21st century."]}, {"id": "king-in-black", "era": "Slott & Allred and after (2014–2026)", "title": "King in Black", "sub": "King in Black #1–5 (2020–2021)", "img": "kib1", "gallery": [], "year": "2020–2021", "dates": "February – June 2021 (cover; #1 released 2 December 2020)", "creators": "Donny Cates (writer), Ryan Stegman (art)", "issues": "King in Black #1–5 and tie-ins", "importance": 3, "syn": ["Knull invades Earth and wraps it in a dome of symbiote darkness. With Earth’s heroes overwhelmed, Thor sends his ravens Hugin and Munin to summon the Silver Surfer, who has seen Knull’s handiwork on world after world. The Surfer frees the Enigma Force – the power behind Captain Universe – from the symbiotes; Knull reels, remembering his ancient defeat at the Surfer’s hands. Channeling the God of Light, the Surfer takes on a chrome form and turns his board into a sword, and the heroes of Earth rally behind him. The final blow belongs to Eddie Brock, who becomes Captain Universe and destroys Knull. Afterwards, Thor and the Surfer reflect that things will not be normal for a long time."], "why": "The pay-off to Silver Surfer: Black and one of the Surfer’s biggest ‘hero moment’ crossovers.", "src": ["Wikipedia: Silver Surfer (2020s; biography)", "Marvel Database: King in Black Vol 1 1"]}, {"id": "cosmic-2018", "era": "Slott & Allred and after (2014–2026)", "title": "Herald and Defender: crossovers 2018–2024", "sub": "Best Defense, Thanos Wins, Thor ‘Black Winter’, Avengers ‘Starbrand Reborn’, Defenders 2021, Fantastic Four ‘Reckoning War’", "img": "thor6_1", "gallery": ["bestdef", "av39", "def6_1", "ff6_46", "thanos13", "icount3", "ssann2_1", "thor6_6", "ds5_15", "ff6_45"], "year": "2018–2024", "dates": "Various, 2018 onward", "creators": "Jason Latour (Silver Surfer: The Best Defense, 2018); Donny Cates (Thanos, 2017–18; Thor, 2020); Jason Aaron (Avengers, 2020–21); Al Ewing & Javier Rodríguez (Defenders, 2021); Dan Slott (Fantastic Four, 2022)", "issues": "Silver Surfer: The Best Defense #1; Thanos vol. 2 #13–18 ‘Thanos Wins’; Thor vol. 6 #1–6 ‘The Devourer King’ (Black Winter); Avengers vol. 8 ‘Starbrand Reborn’; Defenders vol. 6 #1–5; Fantastic Four vol. 6 ‘Reckoning War’", "importance": 2, "syn": ["Silver Surfer: The Best Defense (one-shot, written and drawn by Jason Latour) was part of a 2018 Defenders reunion; Marvel Database lists Thanos Wins (Thanos vol. 2, 2017–18) as featuring an alternate-future Norrin Radd called ‘the Fallen One’, serving King Thanos.", "In Donny Cates’s Thor (2020) Galactus, fleeing the Black Winter that destroyed his previous universe, makes Thor his herald; the Surfer and other heralds help explain the threat to the new All-Father. In Jason Aaron’s Avengers (‘Starbrand Reborn’, 2020–21) the Surfer gathers Firelord and Terrax to hunt the reborn Starbrand, clashes with the Avengers, and then protects the new Starbrand when she gives birth. In Al Ewing and Javier Rodríguez’s Defenders (2021) he rejoins the team on a time-travelling chase into the previous cosmos, where he meets the infant who will become Galactus and tries to teach him compassion. In Dan Slott’s Fantastic Four ‘Reckoning War’ (2022) he gathers the heralds to revive Galactus and helps the FF defeat the cosmic warlord Wrath."], "why": "Shows the Surfer’s modern role: a cosmic ‘first responder’ who moves between the Avengers, the Fantastic Four, Thor and the Defenders. Details here come from Marvel Database plot summaries and are summarised briefly.", "src": ["Marvel Database: Norrin Radd (Earth-616) – Starbrand Reborn, Black Winter, Return to Sixth Cosmos, Reckoning War", "Marvel Database issue pages (Thor Vol 6 1, Defenders Vol 6 1, Thanos Vol 2 13/16)"]}, {"id": "rebirth", "era": "Slott & Allred and after (2014–2026)", "title": "Silver Surfer Rebirth and Rebirth: Legacy", "sub": "Silver Surfer Rebirth #1–5 (2022); Silver Surfer Rebirth: Legacy #1–5 (2023–24)", "img": "ssr1", "gallery": ["ssr5", "ssrl1", "ssrleg1"], "year": "2022–2024", "dates": "Rebirth: March – July 2022 (cover; #1 released 19 Jan 2022); Legacy: from November 2023 (cover; #1 released 6 Sept 2023)", "creators": "Ron Marz (writer), Ron Lim (art) – the 1990s team reunited", "issues": "Silver Surfer Rebirth #1–5; Silver Surfer Rebirth: Legacy #1–5", "importance": 3, "syn": ["Rebirth is set during Marz and Lim’s classic 1990s run. Someone has stolen the Reality Gem, which can grant any wish whatever the consequences, and reality starts bending – including the apparent return of the long-dead Captain Mar-Vell. The Surfer has to team up with his least likely ally, Thanos, to track down the Gem and put reality right. Marvel Database lists the Tyrant, Jack of Hearts and Genis-Vell among the antagonists of the finale, which also includes visions of the life Norrin and Shalla-Bal might have had.", "Legacy continues the throwback: the Surfer takes Mar-Vell’s son Genis-Vell under his wing, but even the Power Cosmic cannot replace a father. The Surfer’s attempt to ease Genis’s grief risks a clash with Adam Warlock’s Infinity Watch, and time-travellers complicate matters."], "why": "A deliberately nostalgic return to the art and tone that defined the character in the 1990s; a good entry point for fans of that era.", "src": ["Marvel Database: Silver Surfer Rebirth Vol 1 1, 5; Silver Surfer Rebirth: Legacy Vol 1 1 (solicitations)", "Wikipedia: Silver Surfer (comic book) – additional series"]}, {"id": "ghost-light", "era": "Slott & Allred and after (2014–2026)", "title": "Silver Surfer: Ghost Light", "sub": "Silver Surfer: Ghost Light #1–5 (2023)", "img": "ssgl1", "gallery": [], "year": "2023", "dates": "April – August 2023 (cover; #1 released 1 Feb 2023)", "creators": "John Jennings (writer), Valentine De Landro (art)", "issues": "#1–5", "importance": 2, "syn": ["Toni Brooks and her family move into her uncle’s old house in Sweetwater, New York. The uncle is Al B. Harper – the physicist who, in Silver Surfer #5 (1968), died disarming the Stranger’s Null-Life Bomb, and over whose grave the Surfer lit an eternal flame. Strange energy fired by Toni and her brother draws the Surfer back to Earth, and Ghost Light turns out to be Al himself.", "Per Marvel Database, the Stranger had secretly taken Al’s life force to his Labworld and erased the event from people’s minds (the ‘Sweetwater Gap’), experimenting on him for over a decade. A stable fragment of the Stranger’s composite mind, the Inner Faction, sent Al back, his nanobots powered by the Surfer’s flame – giving him Power Cosmic-based abilities. A.I.M.’s Scarlet Sector, his former employers, come after him; the deranged Stranger uses Dr. Sombra Solomon as an avatar; and in #5 Al and the Surfer stall the Stranger while Toni and her brother use Al’s device to resync his mind. The Stranger apologises and leaves; Al visits his ex-wife and son.", "Jennings and De Landro use the series to give a 1968 supporting character – one of the first Black characters in Marvel’s cosmic stories, written out by self-sacrifice – a second life, which is what Marvel meant by a hero ‘over 50 years in the making’."], "why": "Brings back Al B. Harper from Silver Surfer #5 as the new hero Ghost Light and revisits the Stranger.", "src": ["Marvel Database: Silver Surfer: Ghost Light Vol 1 1 (solicitation)", "Wikipedia: Silver Surfer (comic book) – additional series"], "deep": []}, {"id": "death-of-surfer", "era": "Slott & Allred and after (2014–2026)", "title": "Death of the Silver Surfer", "sub": "Death of the Silver Surfer #1–5 (2025)", "img": "dss1", "gallery": ["dss5"], "year": "2025", "dates": "August – December 2025 (cover; #1 released 11 June 2025, #5 released 29 Oct 2025)", "creators": "Greg Pak (writer), Sumit Kumar (art), with Tiago Palma (#5)", "issues": "#1–5", "importance": 4, "syn": ["The Surfer returns to defend a war-weary Earth and finds himself targeted by Major Kelly Koh, an agent of the anti-alien organisation B.A.N. He survives her attack and even saves her, which leaves a faint Power Cosmic link between them. B.A.N. then captures him and, it emerges, has been experimenting with the blood of Galactus. The Fantastic Four try and fail to free him.", "Through their link the Surfer slowly works on Koh’s xenophobia. When the blood of Galactus threatens Earth, she frees him in desperation – too late to save her mother. The Surfer gives Koh part of the Power Cosmic, making her a second Silver Surfer, and she places her dying mother in stasis. The two Surfers and the Fantastic Four fight together and Norrin destroys B.A.N.’s station, but B.A.N.’s leader uses the blood to tear a rift in space-time. Norrin enters the rift and sacrifices himself to close it, first passing the Power Cosmic to Koh and asking her to protect life as the Silver Surfer.", "As of the latest material checked for this guide (late 2025 publications), Kelly Koh carries the title. Comic-book deaths are often temporary, so check current Marvel listings for any later developments in 2026."], "why": "The most recent major Surfer story: the death of Norrin Radd and the debut of a new human Silver Surfer.", "src": ["Marvel Database: Death of the Silver Surfer Vol 1 1, 5; Norrin Radd (Earth-616) ‘A New Silver Surfer’"], "deep": ["The B.A.N. leader is named Dennis Hermon on Marvel Database (some news coverage spells it ‘Harmon’). The ending – Norrin entering the space-time rift – is left open-ended in the usual comic-book way."]}, {"id": "alternates", "era": "Alternate versions", "title": "Ultimate, What If? and other alternate Surfers", "sub": "Out-of-continuity versions", "img": "uff42", "gallery": ["ue1", "wi49", "mz1", "ex1", "ux1", "exi29", "thanos16", "glss", "sssup", "unix1"], "year": "1989–2018", "dates": "Various", "creators": "Various (see list)", "issues": "Ultimate Fantastic Four #42–46 (2007); Ultimate Extinction (2006); What If? vol. 2 #49 (1993); Marvel Zombies (2005–06); Earth X / Universe X (1999–2001); Exiles (Earth-552); Last Planet Standing (MC2); Green Lantern/Silver Surfer: Unholy Alliances (1995); Silver Surfer/Superman (1996)", "importance": 3, "syn": ["Ultimate Marvel: Warren Ellis’s Ultimate Extinction (2006) reimagined Galactus as Gah Lak Tus, a swarm-like entity whose shapeshifting silver drones drive populations to mass suicide. Later, in Ultimate Fantastic Four #42–46 (2007, Mike Carey & Pasqual Ferry), Reed accidentally teleports a being he mistakes for a star – Norrin Radd, the ‘Silver Searcher’ – to Earth, triggering chaos. Norrin serves Zenn-La’s ruler Revka Temerlune Edifex Scyros III but was banished for breaking that ruler’s control; when Revka offers to lift the exile in exchange for Earth, Norrin sides with the FF instead and leaves to wander space.", "What If? #49 (1993, Ron Marz & Scott Clark) asks what would happen if the Silver Surfer had seized the Infinity Gauntlet. He uses it for good and creates a world for himself and Shalla-Bal, then destroys the Gauntlet for fear of being corrupted; the couple are presumed dead but live on their new world. Other What If? stories include one in which the Carnage symbiote bonds with the Surfer (What If? #108), ending with the Surfer flying into the sun to destroy it.", "Elsewhere: in Marvel Zombies (2005–06) the zombified heroes devour the Surfer, absorb his cosmic power and go on to kill Galactus. In Earth X and Universe X, Franklin Richards becomes Galactus and Shalla-Bal joins Norrin as a second Silver Surfer. Exiles features a Norrin of Earth-552, a military scientist who destroyed his own world and became Galactus’s herald hoping to restore it. In MC2’s Last Planet Standing he merges with Galactus to undo the damage of a planned new Big Bang. He also met DC’s heroes in Green Lantern/Silver Surfer: Unholy Alliances (1995) and Silver Surfer/Superman (1996). Requiem and Parable are often read as alternate-reality stories too (see their entries)."], "why": "These versions show how flexible the core myth is – the herald’s bargain and the lonely conscience translate into horror, satire and alternate history.", "src": ["Wikipedia: Silver Surfer – Other versions", "Marvel Database issue pages"]}];
